'' =================================================================================================
''
''   File....... RT_power_loss_tests.spin2
''   Purpose.... This object builds, straight on the flash, the block states a power cut can leave: a
''               duplicate ID, an orphan body block, a head block that was programmed and never
''               activated, a block whose CRC no longer matches, and blocks whose CRC matches but
''               whose ID, NextID, EndPtr or data offset is out of range.  For each it checks that
''               can_mount() reports it without changing any block, that mount() repairs it with the
''               right status, that the files which should survive read back whole, and that a second
''               mount is clean.  It also checks that a block move during create_file() leaves the
''               new file whole across a mount, and it injects a flash timeout at each step of a
''               commit in each writer mode and checks that the filesystem recovers from it as
''               it does from a power loss at that step
''   Author..... Stephen M Moraco
''               -- see below for terms of use
''   E-mail.....stephen@ironsheep.biz
''   Started.... NOV 2026
''   Updated.... 24 NOV 2026
''
'' =================================================================================================

{ Each group starts from a fresh format with three files: an untouched two-block file, a two-block
  target file whose blocks the group copies, and a one-block file.  The filesystem is then unmounted
  and the state is built with the driver's block hooks, which change the flash and nothing else.
  Every block built is read back before can_mount() or mount() is called, so a case cannot pass
  when its state was not built.  can_mount() must change no block: every block the group touched is
  fingerprinted before and after it.

  How the driver treats each state, from the three mount passes:
  - a block whose lifecycle is not active (inactive or cancelled) is skipped at once by mount() and
    can_mount(), whatever else it holds: it is never checked, used, cancelled or reported;
  - an active block whose CRC does not match is cancelled by mount(), which reports
    E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, and can_mount() is FALSE;
  - an active block whose CRC matches but whose ID or NextID is not a block ID, whose EndPtr lies
    outside its data area, or whose head data offset skips past its EndPtr, is a bad block too: mount() cancels it and reports E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED,
    can_mount() is FALSE, and an older copy of its ID is kept as the only good one;
  - two active blocks with one ID: mount() keeps the newer lifecycle and cancels the older one,
    returning SUCCESS; can_mount() is FALSE because mount() changes a block;
  - an active block that no file chain reaches (an orphan, or a block of a broken chain) is cancelled
    by mount(), which reports E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, and can_mount() is FALSE.

  So a stop at any step of a file operation leaves one of these states:
  - after an erase, or after any page of a block program: the block is inactive and is ignored;
  - after a body block is activated while its commit chain head is still inactive (write_block(),
    create_file()): the bodies already activated are orphans;
  - after the activate of a replacement, before the old copy is cancelled (rewrite_block(), rename(),
    a block move, a close() or flush() that replaces a head or the last block, a circular trim): a
    duplicate ID, plus orphans where the old copy led to blocks the new one does not;
  - after a cancel in a chain delete or a format: the blocks not yet cancelled that no chain reaches
    are orphans;
  - after the last step of an operation: nothing to repair.

  create_file() writes its head block first and activates it only after the last block, and every block
  it takes for a body block can be a block move.  A move copies an active block with the next lifecycle and
  activates the copy, so a move of the still-inactive head would keep a copy whose CRC is not the one the
  head's activation needs, and the next mount would cancel the file.  The block-move group names one free
  block for the allocations: the head block is written there, and the next allocation picks it again.  The
  file must then mount, read back whole, and leave can_mount() TRUE.

  The fault groups use the driver's flash fault countdown: the Nth flash wait after it is armed reports a
  timeout without polling the chip.  The erase, program or lifecycle command of that wait was already sent,
  so the chip carries it out, and no command after it is sent: the flash is left as a power loss just after
  that step leaves it.  Each group writes a second file and opens it for reading, opens a writer in one mode
  with bytes not yet committed, names one block for the faulted call's one block allocation, so that no
  random block move changes the count of waits, and arms the countdown at the erase, a page program, the
  activate or the cancel of the commit.  A block program is one erase wait and one wait for each of its
  pages; an activate and a cancel are one wait each.  The faulted call must return E_FLASH_TIMEOUT and the
  next call E_NOT_MOUNTED.  The countdown is disarmed before can_mount(), whose first act is a status read
  that waits for the chip to finish, and before the recovering mount().  Every group runs on this one cog,
  so no other cog calls the filesystem while the countdown is armed.

  After the recovering mount() the second file reads back whole, and the writer's file reads back exactly as
  its last commit left it, or exactly with the faulted commit, never a mix of the two:
  - "w" and "a" activate their body blocks as they write them and the commit chain head only at the commit,
    so body blocks activated after the last commit are orphans and are removed;
  - "r+" rewrites a whole block at once, so its file holds either the old copy or the new copy of the block;
  - "w+" writes its head block under the replaced file's head block ID, so one activate replaces that file.

  Three further groups put the fault elsewhere: inside the recovering mount() itself, in can_mount() while
  the filesystem is not mounted, and in the refusal of a block program below the filesystem while it is
  mounted.

  The hook refusal cases run last: a hook that did not check its address would write the flash below
  the filesystem.  They use the activate operation with the inactive lifecycle, which writes a byte of
  all ones, so a refused call that did reach the flash would still clear no bit.  The refusal of a block
  program below the filesystem runs after them, and checks that the sector below the filesystem reads the
  same before and after.
}

CON

     _CLKFREQ        = 320_000_000

{
    ' debug via serial to RPi (using RPi gateway pins)
    '  USES LED1 (56) and LED2 (57) pins
    DEBUG_PIN_TX = 57           ' GRY GPIO 10
    DEBUG_PIN_RX = 56           ' WHT GPIO 8 (gnd is BLK GPIO 6)
    DEBUG_WINDOWS_OFF = -1      ' no debug windows showing
    DEBUG_BAUD = 2_000_000      ' comms at 2Mbit
'}

OBJ
    flash : "flash_fs"
    utils : "RT_utilities"

CON ' ---- Test Geometry ----

    BODY_TAIL_BYTES = 1000                              ' bytes of a two-block file that go into its body block
    FILE_BYTES = flash.BYTES_IN_HEAD_BLOCK + BODY_TAIL_BYTES ' bytes of each two-block file: a full head block and part of a body
    ONE_BLOCK_BYTES = 100                               ' bytes of the one-block file
    FILE_COUNT = 3                                      ' files each group writes
    KEEP_SEED = 1                                       ' first data byte of the untouched file
    TARGET_SEED = 2                                     ' first data byte of the target file
    CRC_SEED = 3                                        ' first data byte of the one-block file
    BYTE_MASK = $FF                                     ' keeps a value to one byte
    MAX_TOUCHED = 4                                     ' blocks one group touches at most
    HEAD_COPY_TOUCH_IDX = 1                             ' entry of the inactive head copy among the blocks its group touches
    NO_BLOCK = -1                                       ' a block address no block has
    FIRST_BLOCK_ADDR = 0                                ' first block address of the filesystem
    BEFORE_FIRST_BLOCK = -1                             ' the block address just below the filesystem
    ORPHAN_ID = flash.BLOCKS - 1                        ' a block ID no block of the three files has
    NOT_A_BLOCK_OP = 0                                  ' an operation the block hook does not have
    NO_SOURCE = 0                                       ' block image address of a hook operation that programs no image
    NO_CYCLE = 0                                        ' lifecycle of a hook operation that writes no lifecycle
    MOVED_FILE_BYTES = flash.BYTES_IN_HEAD_BLOCK + flash.BYTES_IN_BODY_BLOCK + BODY_TAIL_BYTES ' bytes of the file made with create_file(): three blocks
    MOVED_FILL = $6C                                    ' fill value of the file made with create_file()
    OUT_OF_RANGE_ID = flash.BLOCKS                      ' the first value past the last block ID: no table has an entry for it
    BAD_HEAD_END_PTR = flash.ADDR_HEAD_DATA - 1         ' an EndPtr just before the data area of a head block
    BAD_DATA_OFFSET = ONE_BLOCK_BYTES + 1               ' a data offset past the bytes the one-block file holds: its EndPtr falls before the data it skips

    ' The field of a block a bad copy changes:
    '  FIELD_THIS_ID     - the block's own ID
    '  FIELD_NEXT        - the NextID of a block with a next block, the EndPtr of a last block
    '  FIELD_DATA_OFFSET - the data offset of a head block, in the low bits of its filename CRC word
    #0, FIELD_THIS_ID, FIELD_NEXT, FIELD_DATA_OFFSET

CON ' ---- Block Format ----

    ' The first long of a block, and the CRC at its end:
    '  NEXT_TOP..NEXT_BOTTOM   - NextID of a block with more blocks after it, EndPtr of a last block
    '  ID_TOP..ID_BOTTOM       - the block's own ID
    '  CYCLE_TOP..CYCLE_BOTTOM - the lifecycle, in the first byte
    '  BODY_BIT                - 1 in a body block, 0 in a head block
    '  CRC_BYTES               - bytes of the CRC long at the end of the block, which covers the bytes before it
    NEXT_TOP = 31
    NEXT_BOTTOM = 20
    ID_TOP = 19
    ID_BOTTOM = 8
    CYCLE_TOP = 7
    CYCLE_BOTTOM = 5
    BODY_BIT = 1
    CRC_BYTES = 4
    FIRST_DATA_BYTE = 1                                 ' first byte after the byte that holds the lifecycle
    ' The data offset of a head block: the low bits of its filename CRC word, the second long of the block
    DATA_OFFSET_LONG = flash.ADDR_HEAD_FN_CRC / 4       ' the long, counted from 0, that holds the filename CRC word
    DATA_OFFSET_TOP = 11
    DATA_OFFSET_BOTTOM = 0
    BLOCK_CRC_POLY = $AD0424F3 rev 31                   ' polynomial of the block CRC

    ' Lifecycles: an active block has one zero bit, the three active values follow each other in the
    ' order CYCLE_FIRST, CYCLE_SECOND, CYCLE_THIRD, CYCLE_FIRST, and the later one of a pair is the newer;
    ' an inactive block has no zero bit; a cancelled block has two or three
    CYCLE_FIRST = %011
    CYCLE_SECOND = %101
    CYCLE_THIRD = %110
    CYCLE_INACTIVE = %111
    ACTIVE_ONES = 2                                     ' one bits of an active lifecycle
    CANCELLED_MAX_ONES = 1                              ' most one bits of a cancelled lifecycle

CON ' ---- Fault Injection ----

    ' The flash waits of a faulted call, counted from 1 as TEST_armFlashFault() counts them: a block program is
    ' one erase wait and then one wait for each page it programs; an activate and a cancel are one wait each
    WAIT_ERASE = 1                                      ' the erase of the block the call programs
    PAGES_PER_BLOCK = 16                                ' 256-byte pages one block program writes
    PAGE_PICK = 7                                       ' the page, counted from 0, whose wait fails in a page fault: one in the middle of the block
    WAIT_PAGE = WAIT_ERASE + 1 + PAGE_PICK              ' the program of that page
    WAITS_PER_PROGRAM = WAIT_ERASE + PAGES_PER_BLOCK    ' every wait of one block program
    WAIT_ACTIVATE = WAITS_PER_PROGRAM + 1               ' the activate that follows the block program
    WAIT_CANCEL = WAIT_ACTIVATE + 1                     ' the cancel of the old copy, which follows that activate
    WAIT_HEAD_ACTIVATE = WAIT_ACTIVATE + 1              ' the activate of a new file's head block, which follows the activate of its last block
    WAIT_READY_CHECK = 1                                ' the first wait of mount() and of can_mount(): the chip is ready before any block is read
    WAIT_MOUNT_CANCEL = WAIT_READY_CHECK + 1            ' the first cancel mount() makes, after its ready check
    FAULT_DISARM = 0                                    ' the countdown value that injects no fault
    ERASED_LONG = $FFFF_FFFF                            ' a long of the flash that no program has written since its erase
    ONE_REFUSAL = 1                                     ' block programs one refused call adds to the refusal count

    ' The writer modes of the fault cases, and the commit each faulted call makes:
    '  MODE_W_NEW  - "w" on a new name, two blocks given to one write(); close() programs and activates the last
    '                block, then activates the head block that write() programmed
    '  MODE_APPEND - "a" on a one-block file, a flush(), then more bytes; close() programs a new copy of the
    '                block, activates it and cancels the old copy
    '  MODE_R_PLUS - "r+" on a one-block file, every byte rewritten and flushed, then rewritten again; flush()
    '                programs a new copy of the block, activates it and cancels the old copy
    '  MODE_W_PLUS - "w+" on the name of a one-block file; close() programs the new head block under the old
    '                head block's ID, activates it and cancels the old head block
    #0, MODE_W_NEW, MODE_APPEND, MODE_R_PLUS, MODE_W_PLUS

    ' What the writer's file holds after the recovering mount():
    '  WRITER_GONE - there is no file of its name: nothing of it was committed
    '  WRITER_OLD  - exactly its contents as of its last commit before the fault
    '  WRITER_NEW  - exactly its contents with the faulted commit
    #0, WRITER_GONE, WRITER_OLD, WRITER_NEW

    ' What the block the faulted call programmed holds once the chip is ready:
    '  NAMED_NOT_CHECKED     - the case checks the block only through the files
    '  NAMED_ERASED          - erased whole: the fault stopped the block program after its erase
    '  NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED - inactive, its first page programmed and its last page still erased
    #0, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED, NAMED_ERASED, NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED

    ' The block the faulted call's one block allocation takes:
    '  TAKE_FREE_BLOCK  - the lowest free block, named so that the allocation moves no block
    '  MOVE_SECOND_HEAD - the second file's head block, named so that the allocation moves it to a free block
    #0, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, MOVE_SECOND_HEAD

    SECOND_SEED = 4                                     ' first data byte of the second file
    WRITER_SEED = 5                                     ' first data byte of the writer's file in "w" and "a"
    APPEND_BASE_BYTES = 1000                            ' bytes of the file "a" opens
    APPEND_FIRST_BYTES = 500                            ' bytes "a" writes and flushes
    APPEND_LAST_BYTES = 500                             ' bytes "a" writes after the flush
    REWRITE_BYTES = 2000                                ' bytes of the one-block file "r+" and "w+" open
    RP_OPEN_SEED = 6                                    ' first data byte of the file "r+" opens
    RP_FLUSH_SEED = 7                                   ' first data byte of the rewrite "r+" flushes
    RP_FAULT_SEED = 8                                   ' first data byte of the rewrite whose flush is faulted
    WP_OLD_SEED = 9                                     ' first data byte of the file "w+" replaces
    WP_NEW_SEED = 10                                    ' first data byte of the file "w+" writes
    WP_NEW_BYTES = 1500                                 ' bytes "w+" writes
    FILES_WITH_WRITER = 2                               ' files of a fault case when the writer's file is kept
    FILES_WITHOUT_WRITER = 1                            ' files of a fault case when only the second file is kept

DAT ' ---- Files ----

keepFile        BYTE    "pl_keep.bin", 0                ' a two-block file no case touches
targetFile      BYTE    "pl_target.bin", 0              ' a two-block file whose blocks the cases copy
crcFile         BYTE    "pl_crc.bin", 0                 ' a one-block file whose block one case corrupts
movedFile       BYTE    "pl_moved.bin", 0               ' a three-block file made with create_file() while a block is named
secondFile      BYTE    "pl_second.bin", 0              ' a two-block file open for reading at each fault
writerFile      BYTE    "pl_writer.bin", 0              ' the file the writer has open at each fault

DAT ' ---- Block Addresses and State ----

bSetUp          LONG    FALSE                           ' TRUE when the group's files were written and their blocks found
targetHeadAddr  LONG    0                               ' head block of the target file
targetBodyAddr  LONG    0                               ' body block of the target file
crcHeadAddr     LONG    0                               ' the one block of the one-block file
targetHeadId    LONG    0                               ' block ID of the target file's head block
targetBodyId    LONG    0                               ' block ID of the target file's body block
copyAddr        LONG    0                               ' block a case programmed
sourceAddr      LONG    0                               ' block a case copied into copyAddr with one field changed
secondCopyAddr  LONG    0                               ' second block a case programmed
copyCycle       LONG    0                               ' lifecycle the copy was given
touchedCount    LONG    0                               ' blocks in touchedAddr
touchedAddr     LONG    0[MAX_TOUCHED]                  ' blocks the group touched or copied
printsBefore    LONG    0[MAX_TOUCHED]                  ' fingerprint of each touched block before can_mount()
printsAfter     LONG    0[MAX_TOUCHED]                  ' fingerprint of each touched block after can_mount()
writerHndl      LONG    0                               ' handle of the writer a fault case opens
secondHndl      LONG    0                               ' handle of the second file, open for reading
faultBlockAddr  LONG    0                               ' block named for the faulted call's block allocation
oldBytes        LONG    0                               ' bytes of the writer's file as of its last commit
oldSeed         LONG    0                               ' first data byte of the writer's file as of its last commit
newBytes        LONG    0                               ' bytes of the writer's file with the faulted commit
newSeed         LONG    0                               ' first data byte of the writer's file with the faulted commit

DAT ' ---- Buffers ----

dataBuf         BYTE    0[FILE_BYTES]                   ' what a file is written from and read into
sourceBuf       BYTE    0[flash.BLOCK_SIZE]             ' the block a case copies, as read before the copy
copyBuf         BYTE    0[flash.BLOCK_SIZE]             ' the image a case programs


PUB go() | status, pFailedCall
'' Run every power-loss state test, then show the result.
''

' Local Variables:
' @local status - status of the format and mount that start the run
' @local pFailedCall - address of the name of the call that failed

    pFailedCall := @"format()"
    status := flash.format()      ' Comment out this line to not erase all files
    if status == flash.SUCCESS
        pFailedCall := @"mount()"
        status := flash.mount()

    if status == flash.SUCCESS
        utils.checkDriverInstance(flash.MAX_FILES_OPEN)

        utils.showChipAndDriverVersion()
        utils.setCheckCountPerTest(1)                   ' each check of a case is shown on its own

        testDuplicateId()
        testOrphanBody()
        testInactiveHead()
        testBadCrcBlock()
        testOutOfRangeId()
        testOutOfRangeNextId()
        testOutOfRangeEndPtr()
        testOutOfRangeDataOffset()
        testMovedNewHead()
        testFaults()
        testRecoveryFaults()
        testUnmountedFault()
        debug("* The next calls give the block hooks addresses outside the filesystem")
        testHookRefusals()
        testGuardRefusal()
    else
        utils.showError(pFailedCall, status)
        utils.startTest(@"format() and mount()")        ' a filesystem that did not mount is a failed run
        utils.evaluateResultNegError(status, flash.SUCCESS)

    debug("* Test Complete")
    utils.ShowTestResult(@"RT_power_loss_tests")


PRI testDuplicateId()
' A copy of the target file's body block with the next lifecycle: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() keeps the newer copy, cancels the older one and returns SUCCESS.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a duplicate ID: a copy of a body block with the next lifecycle")
    setUpFiles()
    buildDuplicate()
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for a duplicate ID and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountDuplicate()
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI buildDuplicate() | pBlock, status, actStatus
' Copy the target file's body block to a free block with the next lifecycle, activate it and read it back, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of a block read back
' @local status - what the program operation returned
' @local actStatus - what the activate operation returned

    utils.startTest(@"copy the body block of the target file, activate the copy with the next lifecycle and read it back")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        readInto(targetBodyAddr, @sourceBuf)
        copyCycle := nextCycle(cycleOf(@sourceBuf))
        copyAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
        bytemove(@copyBuf, @sourceBuf, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, copyAddr, @copyBuf, copyCycle)
        actStatus := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, copyAddr, NO_SOURCE, copyCycle)
        debug("  body block ", uhex_word_(targetBodyAddr), " copied to block ", uhex_word_(copyAddr))
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"program the copy", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(actStatus, @"activate the copy", flash.SUCCESS)

        pBlock := readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the copy was read back", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"lifecycle of the copy is the next one", copyCycle)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(idOf(@copyBuf), @"block ID of the copy is the body block's", targetBodyId)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@copyBuf), @"the copy's CRC matches", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(dataMatches(@copyBuf, @sourceBuf), @"the copy holds the body block's data", TRUE)

        readInto(targetBodyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the body block keeps its lifecycle", cycleOf(@sourceBuf))
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isActive(cycleOf(@sourceBuf)), @"the body block is active", TRUE)

        setTouched(targetBodyAddr, copyAddr, NO_BLOCK, NO_BLOCK)


PRI mountDuplicate()
' mount() resolves the duplicate silently: SUCCESS, the older copy cancelled and the newer one kept, as one test.

    utils.startTest(@"mount() cancels the older copy, keeps the newer one and returns SUCCESS")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        mountAndCheck(flash.SUCCESS)                    ' a duplicate ID is repaired without a status
        readInto(targetBodyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCancelled(cycleOf(@copyBuf)), @"the older copy is cancelled", TRUE)
        readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the newer copy keeps its lifecycle", copyCycle)


PRI testOrphanBody()
' A copy of the target file's body block under an ID no file uses: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() cancels it and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"an orphan body block: an active body block no file reaches")
    setUpFiles()
    buildOrphan()
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for an orphan body block and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountOrphan()
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI buildOrphan() | pBlock, status, actStatus
' Copy the target file's body block to a free block under an unused ID, activate it and read it back, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of a block read back
' @local status - what the program operation returned
' @local actStatus - what the activate operation returned

    utils.startTest(@"copy the body block of the target file under an unused ID, activate it and read it back")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        readInto(targetBodyAddr, @sourceBuf)
        copyCycle := CYCLE_FIRST                        ' the lifecycle a new body block is given
        copyAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
        bytemove(@copyBuf, @sourceBuf, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
        LONG[@copyBuf].[ID_TOP..ID_BOTTOM] := ORPHAN_ID
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, copyAddr, @copyBuf, copyCycle)
        actStatus := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, copyAddr, NO_SOURCE, copyCycle)
        debug("  orphan body block at block ", uhex_word_(copyAddr))
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"program the orphan", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(actStatus, @"activate the orphan", flash.SUCCESS)

        pBlock := readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the orphan was read back", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"lifecycle of the orphan", copyCycle)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(idOf(@copyBuf), @"block ID of the orphan", ORPHAN_ID)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(LONG[@copyBuf].[BODY_BIT], @"the orphan is a body block", 1)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@copyBuf), @"the orphan's CRC matches", TRUE)

        setTouched(copyAddr, targetBodyAddr, NO_BLOCK, NO_BLOCK)


PRI mountOrphan()
' mount() cancels the orphan and reports it, as one test.

    utils.startTest(@"mount() cancels the orphan body block and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        mountAndCheck(flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED)       ' an active block no file reaches is cancelled and reported
        readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCancelled(cycleOf(@copyBuf)), @"the orphan is cancelled", TRUE)


PRI testInactiveHead()
' A copy of the target file's head block with the next lifecycle, programmed and never activated, leading to an active body block: the head copy is ignored, the body copy is an orphan.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a head block programmed and never activated, with the body block it leads to")
    setUpFiles()
    buildInactiveHead()
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is TRUE while the one change is a head block that was never activated, and changes no block", TRUE)
    buildInactiveHeadBody()
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE once the body block the inactive head leads to is active, and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountInactiveHead()
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI buildInactiveHead() | pBlock, status
' Copy the target file's head block to a free block with the next lifecycle and a NextID no block has, program it without activating it and read it back, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of a block read back
' @local status - what the program operation returned

    utils.startTest(@"copy the head block of the target file with the next lifecycle, never activate it, and read it back")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        readInto(targetHeadAddr, @sourceBuf)
        copyCycle := nextCycle(cycleOf(@sourceBuf))
        copyAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
        bytemove(@copyBuf, @sourceBuf, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
        LONG[@copyBuf].[NEXT_TOP..NEXT_BOTTOM] := ORPHAN_ID
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, copyAddr, @copyBuf, copyCycle)
        debug("  inactive head block at block ", uhex_word_(copyAddr))
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"program the head copy", flash.SUCCESS)

        pBlock := readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the head copy was read back", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the head copy is inactive", CYCLE_INACTIVE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(idOf(@copyBuf), @"block ID of the head copy is the head block's", targetHeadId)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(LONG[@copyBuf].[NEXT_TOP..NEXT_BOTTOM], @"NextID of the head copy", ORPHAN_ID)
        BYTE[@copyBuf].[CYCLE_TOP..CYCLE_BOTTOM] := copyCycle
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@copyBuf), @"the head copy's CRC matches once activated with the next lifecycle", TRUE)

        setTouched(targetHeadAddr, copyAddr, NO_BLOCK, NO_BLOCK)


PRI buildInactiveHeadBody() | pBlock, status, actStatus
' Copy the target file's body block, with the ID the inactive head leads to and one data byte changed, to a free block, activate it and read it back, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of a block read back
' @local status - what the program operation returned
' @local actStatus - what the activate operation returned

    utils.startTest(@"copy the body block under the ID the inactive head leads to, with one data byte changed, activate it and read it back")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        readInto(targetBodyAddr, @sourceBuf)
        secondCopyAddr := findFreeBlock(copyAddr)
        bytemove(@copyBuf, @sourceBuf, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
        LONG[@copyBuf].[ID_TOP..ID_BOTTOM] := ORPHAN_ID
        BYTE[@copyBuf][flash.ADDR_BODY_DATA] ^= BYTE_MASK  ' a file that read this block would read a byte the file never held
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, secondCopyAddr, @copyBuf, CYCLE_FIRST)
        actStatus := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, secondCopyAddr, NO_SOURCE, CYCLE_FIRST)
        debug("  body block the inactive head leads to at block ", uhex_word_(secondCopyAddr))
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"program the body copy", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(actStatus, @"activate the body copy", flash.SUCCESS)

        pBlock := readInto(secondCopyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the body copy was read back", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"lifecycle of the body copy", CYCLE_FIRST)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(idOf(@copyBuf), @"block ID of the body copy", ORPHAN_ID)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@copyBuf), @"the body copy's CRC matches", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(BYTE[@copyBuf][flash.ADDR_BODY_DATA] == BYTE[@sourceBuf][flash.ADDR_BODY_DATA], @"the body copy's first data byte differs from the body block's", FALSE)

        setTouched(targetHeadAddr, copyAddr, targetBodyAddr, secondCopyAddr)


PRI mountInactiveHead() | printNow
' mount() cancels the active body copy, reports it, and leaves the inactive head copy as it was, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local printNow - fingerprint of the inactive head copy after mount()

    utils.startTest(@"mount() cancels the body copy, returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED and leaves the inactive head copy as it was")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        mountAndCheck(flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED)       ' the active body copy is reached by no active head
        readInto(secondCopyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCancelled(cycleOf(@copyBuf)), @"the body copy is cancelled", TRUE)
        readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the head copy is still inactive", CYCLE_INACTIVE)
        printNow := blockPrint(copyAddr)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(printNow, @"the head copy is unchanged by mount()", printsAfter[HEAD_COPY_TOUCH_IDX])


PRI testBadCrcBlock()
' The one-block file's block with a reserved bit cleared, so its CRC no longer matches: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() cancels it and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"an active block whose CRC no longer matches")
    setUpFiles()
    buildBadCrc()
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for a block whose CRC does not match and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountBadCrc()
    survivorsStep(FALSE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI buildBadCrc() | pBlock, status
' Clear the reserved bit of the one-block file's block and read the block back, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of a block read back
' @local status - what the corrupt operation returned

    utils.startTest(@"clear a reserved bit of the one-block file's block and read it back")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        readInto(crcHeadAddr, @sourceBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(BYTE[@sourceBuf] & flash.TEST_CORRUPT_BIT, @"the reserved bit is set before the change", flash.TEST_CORRUPT_BIT)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@sourceBuf), @"the block's CRC matches before the change", TRUE)
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_CORRUPT, crcHeadAddr, NO_SOURCE, NO_CYCLE)
        debug("  block ", uhex_word_(crcHeadAddr), " corrupted")
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"clear the reserved bit", flash.SUCCESS)

        pBlock := readInto(crcHeadAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the block was read back", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(BYTE[@copyBuf], @"first byte of the block lost the reserved bit", BYTE[@sourceBuf] & !flash.TEST_CORRUPT_BIT & BYTE_MASK)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the block keeps its lifecycle", cycleOf(@sourceBuf))
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isActive(cycleOf(@copyBuf)), @"the block is active", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@copyBuf), @"the block's CRC matches after the change", FALSE)

        setTouched(crcHeadAddr, NO_BLOCK, NO_BLOCK, NO_BLOCK)


PRI mountBadCrc()
' mount() cancels the block whose CRC does not match and reports it, as one test.

    utils.startTest(@"mount() cancels the block whose CRC does not match and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        mountAndCheck(flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED)       ' a block cancelled for its CRC is a bad block removed
        readInto(crcHeadAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCancelled(cycleOf(@copyBuf)), @"the block is cancelled", TRUE)


PRI testOutOfRangeId()
' A copy of the target file's body block whose ID is past the last block ID, its CRC good: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() cancels it as a bad block and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, and every file survives.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a block whose ID is past the last block ID")
    setUpFiles()
    buildFieldCopy(@"copy the body block of the target file with an ID past the last one, activate it and read it back", targetBodyAddr, FIELD_THIS_ID, OUT_OF_RANGE_ID)
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for a block whose ID is past the last one and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountFieldCopy(@"mount() cancels the block whose ID is past the last one and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED")
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI testOutOfRangeNextId()
' A copy of the target file's head block with the next lifecycle and a NextID past the last block ID, its CRC good: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() cancels the copy as a bad block, keeps the older head and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, and every file survives.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a newer head block whose NextID is past the last block ID")
    setUpFiles()
    buildFieldCopy(@"copy the head block of the target file with the next lifecycle and a NextID past the last block ID, activate it and read it back", targetHeadAddr, FIELD_NEXT, OUT_OF_RANGE_ID)
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for a block whose NextID is past the last block ID and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountFieldCopy(@"mount() cancels the head block whose NextID is past the last block ID, keeps the older head and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED")
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI testOutOfRangeEndPtr()
' A copy of the one-block file's block with the next lifecycle and an EndPtr before its data area, its CRC good: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() cancels the copy as a bad block, keeps the older block and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, and every file survives.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a newer last block whose EndPtr lies before its data area")
    setUpFiles()
    buildFieldCopy(@"copy the one-block file's block with the next lifecycle and an EndPtr before its data area, activate it and read it back", crcHeadAddr, FIELD_NEXT, BAD_HEAD_END_PTR)
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for a block whose EndPtr lies before its data area and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountFieldCopy(@"mount() cancels the block whose EndPtr lies before its data area, keeps the older block and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED")
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI testOutOfRangeDataOffset()
' A copy of the one-block file's block with the next lifecycle and a data offset past the bytes it holds, its CRC good: can_mount() is FALSE, mount() cancels the copy as a bad block, keeps the older block and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, and every file survives.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a newer head block whose data offset skips past its EndPtr")
    setUpFiles()
    buildFieldCopy(@"copy the one-block file's block with the next lifecycle and a data offset past the bytes it holds, activate it and read it back", crcHeadAddr, FIELD_DATA_OFFSET, BAD_DATA_OFFSET)
    canMountStep(@"can_mount() is FALSE for a head block whose data offset skips past its EndPtr and changes no block", FALSE)
    mountFieldCopy(@"mount() cancels the head block whose data offset skips past its EndPtr, keeps the older block and returns E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED")
    survivorsStep(TRUE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI buildFieldCopy(pTitle, copiedAddr, fieldSel, fieldValue) | pBlock, status, actStatus
' Copy a block of the files to a free block with the next lifecycle and one control field changed, activate the copy and read it back, as one test.

' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test
' @param copiedAddr - the block to copy
' @param fieldSel - the field the copy changes: FIELD_THIS_ID, FIELD_NEXT or FIELD_DATA_OFFSET
' @param fieldValue - the value the copy holds in that field

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of a block read back
' @local status - what the program operation returned
' @local actStatus - what the activate operation returned

    utils.startTest(pTitle)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        sourceAddr := copiedAddr
        readInto(sourceAddr, @sourceBuf)
        copyCycle := nextCycle(cycleOf(@sourceBuf))
        copyAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
        bytemove(@copyBuf, @sourceBuf, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
        setField(@copyBuf, fieldSel, fieldValue)
        ' programming computes the CRC over the changed field, so only the field is out of range
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, copyAddr, @copyBuf, copyCycle)
        actStatus := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, copyAddr, NO_SOURCE, copyCycle)
        debug("  block ", uhex_word_(sourceAddr), " copied to block ", uhex_word_(copyAddr), " with a field set to ", uhex_word_(fieldValue))
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"program the copy", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(actStatus, @"activate the copy", flash.SUCCESS)

        pBlock := readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the copy was read back", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"lifecycle of the copy is the next one", copyCycle)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(fieldOf(@copyBuf, fieldSel), @"the copy holds the changed field", fieldValue)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCrcGood(@copyBuf), @"the copy's CRC matches", TRUE)

        readInto(sourceAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the copied block keeps its lifecycle", cycleOf(@sourceBuf))
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isActive(cycleOf(@sourceBuf)), @"the copied block is active", TRUE)

        setTouched(sourceAddr, copyAddr, NO_BLOCK, NO_BLOCK)


PRI mountFieldCopy(pTitle)
' mount() cancels the copy with the field out of range, reports it, and keeps the block it was copied from, as one test.

' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test

    utils.startTest(pTitle)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        mountAndCheck(flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED)       ' a block with a field out of range is a bad block removed
        readInto(copyAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(isCancelled(cycleOf(@copyBuf)), @"the copy is cancelled", TRUE)
        readInto(sourceAddr, @copyBuf)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the copied block keeps its lifecycle", cycleOf(@sourceBuf))


PRI testMovedNewHead()
' create_file() with one free block named for the allocations: its head block is written there and picked again while still inactive; the file must survive a mount whole.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a block move during create_file() and the new file's inactive head block")
    createWithNamedBlock()
    movedHeadMountStep()


PRI createWithNamedBlock() | status, namedAddr, setStatus, clearStatus, headAddr
' Format, name a free block for the allocations, make a three-block file with create_file() and clear the name, as one test; bSetUp tells the next step whether it worked.

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what format() and create_file() returned
' @local namedAddr - the free block named for the allocations
' @local setStatus - what naming the block returned
' @local clearStatus - what clearing the name returned
' @local headAddr - the block that holds the new file's head block after create_file()

    utils.startTest(@"create_file() of a three-block file while the block allocations are named to take one free block")
    bSetUp := FALSE
    status := flash.format()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"format()", flash.SUCCESS)
    namedAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.TEST_blockStateOf(namedAddr), @"the named block is free", flash.B_FREE)

    setStatus := flash.TEST_setTargetBlock(namedAddr)
    ' the head block is written to the free named block, and each body block's allocation picks the named block again
    status := flash.create_file(@movedFile, MOVED_FILL, MOVED_FILE_BYTES)
    clearStatus := flash.TEST_setTargetBlock(flash.NOT_ENABLED)
    _, _, headAddr, _ := flash.TEST_get4kBlockAndNextAtOffset(@movedFile, 0)
    debug("  named block ", uhex_word_(namedAddr), ", the new file's head block is at block ", uhex_word_(headAddr))

    utils.evaluateSubStatus(setStatus, @"name the block", flash.SUCCESS)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"create_file()", flash.SUCCESS)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(clearStatus, @"clear the name", flash.SUCCESS)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.TEST_blockStateOf(namedAddr) <> flash.B_FREE, @"the named block holds a block of the new file", TRUE)
    bSetUp := status == flash.SUCCESS and setStatus == flash.SUCCESS and clearStatus == flash.SUCCESS


PRI movedHeadMountStep() | status
' Unmount, then check can_mount() is TRUE, mount() returns SUCCESS and the new file reads back whole, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what unmount() returned

    utils.startTest(@"the file made while a block was named survives an unmount and a mount, and reads back whole")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the file was made", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        status := flash.unmount()
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"unmount()", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.can_mount(), @"can_mount()", TRUE)
        mountAndCheck(flash.SUCCESS)                    ' a head block whose CRC matches its active lifecycle keeps the whole file
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.exists(@movedFile), @"the file exists", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(fillMatches(@movedFile, MOVED_FILE_BYTES, MOVED_FILL), @"the file reads back whole", TRUE)


PRI testHookRefusals()
' The block hooks refuse the block addresses just outside the filesystem and an operation they do not have, and accept the addresses inside it.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"the block hooks refuse an address outside the filesystem")
    refuseChange()
    refuseRead()
    acceptInside()


PRI refuseChange()
' TEST_changeBlock() refuses -1, BLOCKS and an operation it does not have, as one test.

    utils.startTest(@"TEST_changeBlock() refuses block addresses -1 and BLOCKS and an operation it does not have")
    ' each call below is refused before any flash command is sent
    checkStatus(flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, BEFORE_FIRST_BLOCK, NO_SOURCE, CYCLE_INACTIVE), flash.E_TEST_BAD_BLOCK_ARG)
    checkStatus(flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, flash.BLOCKS, NO_SOURCE, CYCLE_INACTIVE), flash.E_TEST_BAD_BLOCK_ARG)
    checkStatus(flash.TEST_changeBlock(NOT_A_BLOCK_OP, FIRST_BLOCK_ADDR, NO_SOURCE, CYCLE_INACTIVE), flash.E_TEST_BAD_BLOCK_ARG)


PRI refuseRead()
' TEST_readBlock() refuses -1 and BLOCKS, as one test.

    utils.startTest(@"TEST_readBlock() refuses block addresses -1 and BLOCKS")
    ' a refused read returns the error code instead of a buffer address
    checkStatus(flash.TEST_readBlock(BEFORE_FIRST_BLOCK), flash.E_TEST_BAD_BLOCK_ARG)
    checkStatus(flash.TEST_readBlock(flash.BLOCKS), flash.E_TEST_BAD_BLOCK_ARG)


PRI acceptInside() | freeAddr, printBefore, printNow, status, errCode
' The hooks accept the first and last block addresses, and an activate with the inactive lifecycle leaves a free block as it was, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local freeAddr - a block no file uses
' @local printBefore - fingerprint of that block before the activate
' @local printNow - fingerprint of that block after it
' @local status - what the activate returned
' @local errCode - error() after the activate

    utils.startTest(@"the block hooks accept block addresses 0 and BLOCKS - 1")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.TEST_readBlock(FIRST_BLOCK_ADDR) > 0, @"TEST_readBlock(0) returns a buffer", TRUE)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(flash.error(), @"error() after TEST_readBlock(0)", flash.SUCCESS)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.TEST_readBlock(flash.BLOCKS - 1) > 0, @"TEST_readBlock(BLOCKS - 1) returns a buffer", TRUE)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(flash.error(), @"error() after TEST_readBlock(BLOCKS - 1)", flash.SUCCESS)

    freeAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
    printBefore := blockPrint(freeAddr)
    status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_ACTIVATE, freeAddr, NO_SOURCE, CYCLE_INACTIVE)
    errCode := flash.error()
    printNow := blockPrint(freeAddr)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"activate a free block with the inactive lifecycle", flash.SUCCESS)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after the activate", flash.SUCCESS)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(printNow, @"the free block is unchanged", printBefore)


PRI testFaults()
' Inject a flash timeout at each stopping point of the commit in each writer mode, with a second file open for reading.

    ' A block that was erased, or programmed only in part, is inactive: mount() and can_mount() skip it, and it
    ' stays on the flash as it is. Nothing of a new "w" file is active before its last block is activated.
    faultCase(@"w new file: the erase of the last block fails in close()", MODE_W_NEW, WAIT_ERASE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_GONE, NAMED_ERASED)
    faultCase(@"w new file: a page program of the last block fails in close()", MODE_W_NEW, WAIT_PAGE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_GONE, NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED)
    ' The activate's command was sent before its wait failed, so the chip carries it out. An active body block
    ' whose head block is not active is reached by no file: mount() cancels it and reports it.
    faultCase(@"w new file: the activate of the last block fails in close()", MODE_W_NEW, WAIT_ACTIVATE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, FALSE, flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED, WRITER_GONE, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)
    ' Once the head block's activate is carried out the file is whole on the flash.
    faultCase(@"w new file: the activate of the head block fails in close()", MODE_W_NEW, WAIT_HEAD_ACTIVATE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)
    ' The allocation moves the second file's head block first: the copy is programmed and activated and the
    ' original is cancelled. The cancel is carried out, so the move is complete and close() wrote nothing.
    faultCase(@"w new file: the cancel of a block move fails in close()", MODE_W_NEW, WAIT_CANCEL, MOVE_SECOND_HEAD, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_GONE, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)

    ' Until the new copy is active the old copy is the file. Once it is active there are two active blocks with
    ' one ID: mount() keeps the newer and cancels the older, returning SUCCESS, and can_mount() is FALSE because
    ' that mount() changes a block. Once the old copy's cancel is carried out nothing is left to repair.
    faultCase(@"a: the erase of the new copy of the last block fails in close()", MODE_APPEND, WAIT_ERASE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_OLD, NAMED_ERASED)
    faultCase(@"a: a page program of the new copy of the last block fails in close()", MODE_APPEND, WAIT_PAGE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_OLD, NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED)
    faultCase(@"a: the activate of the new copy fails in close()", MODE_APPEND, WAIT_ACTIVATE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, FALSE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)
    faultCase(@"a: the cancel of the old copy fails in close()", MODE_APPEND, WAIT_CANCEL, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)

    ' The same three states for the rewrite of one block: the file holds the old copy whole or the new copy whole.
    faultCase(@"r+: the erase of the new copy of the block fails in flush()", MODE_R_PLUS, WAIT_ERASE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_OLD, NAMED_ERASED)
    faultCase(@"r+: a page program of the new copy of the block fails in flush()", MODE_R_PLUS, WAIT_PAGE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_OLD, NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED)
    faultCase(@"r+: the activate of the new copy fails in flush()", MODE_R_PLUS, WAIT_ACTIVATE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, FALSE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)
    faultCase(@"r+: the cancel of the old copy fails in flush()", MODE_R_PLUS, WAIT_CANCEL, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)

    ' The same three states for the head block of the file a "w+" commit replaces.
    faultCase(@"w+ over a file: the erase of the new head block fails in close()", MODE_W_PLUS, WAIT_ERASE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_OLD, NAMED_ERASED)
    faultCase(@"w+ over a file: a page program of the new head block fails in close()", MODE_W_PLUS, WAIT_PAGE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_OLD, NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED)
    faultCase(@"w+ over a file: the activate of the new head block fails in close()", MODE_W_PLUS, WAIT_ACTIVATE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, FALSE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)
    faultCase(@"w+ over a file: the cancel of the old head block fails in close()", MODE_W_PLUS, WAIT_CANCEL, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK, TRUE, flash.SUCCESS, WRITER_NEW, NAMED_NOT_CHECKED)


PRI faultCase(pTitle, writerMode, waitNumber, allocation, bCanMount, mountStatus, writerOutcome, namedCheck)
' Run one fault case as a test group: set up the writer and the second file, inject the fault, recover and check the files.

' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test
' @param writerMode - the writer's mode: MODE_W_NEW, MODE_APPEND, MODE_R_PLUS or MODE_W_PLUS
' @param waitNumber - the flash wait of the faulted call that fails, counted from 1
' @param allocation - the block the faulted call's allocation takes: TAKE_FREE_BLOCK or MOVE_SECOND_HEAD
' @param bCanMount - the answer can_mount() must give after the fault
' @param mountStatus - the status the recovering mount() must return
' @param writerOutcome - what the writer's file must hold: WRITER_GONE, WRITER_OLD or WRITER_NEW
' @param namedCheck - what the programmed block must hold: NAMED_NOT_CHECKED, NAMED_ERASED or NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED

    utils.startTestGroup(pTitle)
    setUpWriter(writerMode)
    injectFault(writerMode, waitNumber, allocation)
    recoverStep(bCanMount, mountStatus, namedCheck)
    committedStep(writerOutcome)
    secondMountStep()


PRI testRecoveryFaults()
' Inject a flash timeout inside the recovering mount(), after the fault that leaves an orphan body block.

    ' The ready check fails before any block is read, so the orphan is still there for the next mount().
    recoveryFaultCase(@"a fault in the ready check of the recovering mount()", WAIT_READY_CHECK, flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED)
    ' The orphan's cancel was sent before its wait failed, so the chip carries it out: the next mount() finds
    ' nothing to remove.
    recoveryFaultCase(@"a fault in the cancel of the orphan body block by the recovering mount()", WAIT_MOUNT_CANCEL, flash.SUCCESS)


PRI recoveryFaultCase(pTitle, recoveryWait, retryStatus)
' Run one fault in recovery as a test group: a "w" close() whose last block's activate fails, then a mount() whose own wait fails.

' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test
' @param recoveryWait - the flash wait of mount() that fails, counted from 1
' @param retryStatus - the status the next mount() must return

    utils.startTestGroup(pTitle)
    setUpWriter(MODE_W_NEW)
    injectFault(MODE_W_NEW, WAIT_ACTIVATE, TAKE_FREE_BLOCK)
    faultedMountStep(recoveryWait, retryStatus)
    committedStep(WRITER_GONE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI testUnmountedFault()
' Inject a flash timeout into can_mount() while the filesystem is not mounted.

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a flash wait that fails in can_mount() while the filesystem is not mounted")
    setUpSecond(FALSE)
    unmountedFaultStep()
    committedStep(WRITER_GONE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI testGuardRefusal()
' Refuse a block program below the filesystem while it is mounted, through each of the two programming primitives.

    guardCase(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, @"the block program refused below the filesystem returns E_FLASH_TIMEOUT and unmounts")
    guardCase(flash.TEST_BLK_CORRUPT, @"the lifecycle program refused below the filesystem returns E_FLASH_TIMEOUT and unmounts")


PRI guardCase(blockOp, pTitle)
' Run one refusal as a test group: mount with the second file open for reading, refuse the program, recover and check the file.

' @param blockOp - the primitive TEST_programBelowFs() calls: flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM or flash.TEST_BLK_CORRUPT
' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test

    utils.startTestGroup(@"a block program refused below the filesystem while it is mounted")
    setUpSecond(TRUE)
    guardRefusalStep(blockOp, pTitle)
    committedStep(WRITER_GONE)
    secondMountStep()


PRI setUpWriter(writerMode) | status, bSecond, bBase, bOpen, bWriter
' Format, write the second file and the file the writer opens, open the second file for reading and open the writer with bytes not yet committed, as one test; bSetUp tells the later steps whether it worked.

' @param writerMode - the writer's mode: MODE_W_NEW, MODE_APPEND, MODE_R_PLUS or MODE_W_PLUS

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what format() returned
' @local bSecond - TRUE when the second file was written
' @local bBase - TRUE when the file the writer opens was written, or the mode opens no file
' @local bOpen - TRUE when the second file is open for reading
' @local bWriter - TRUE when the writer is open with bytes not yet committed

    utils.startTest(@"set up: write the second file and the writer's file, open the second file for reading and open the writer")
    bSetUp := FALSE
    status := flash.format()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"format()", flash.SUCCESS)
    bSecond := writeFile(@secondFile, FILE_BYTES, SECOND_SEED)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSecond, @"the second file is written", TRUE)
    bBase := writeWriterBase(writerMode)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bBase, @"the file the writer opens is written", TRUE)
    secondHndl := flash.open(@secondFile, flash.FILEMODE_READ)
    bOpen := isUsableHandle(secondHndl)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bOpen, @"the second file is open for reading", TRUE)

    case writerMode
        MODE_W_NEW: bWriter := openNewWriter()
        MODE_APPEND: bWriter := openAppendWriter()
        MODE_R_PLUS: bWriter := openRewriteWriter()
        other: bWriter := openReplaceWriter()
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bWriter, @"the writer is open with bytes not yet committed", TRUE)
    bSetUp := status == flash.SUCCESS and bSecond and bBase and bOpen and bWriter


PRI writeWriterBase(writerMode) : bWritten
' Write the file the writer opens: none for "w" on a new name, a one-block file for the other modes.

' @param writerMode - the writer's mode: MODE_W_NEW, MODE_APPEND, MODE_R_PLUS or MODE_W_PLUS
' @returns bWritten - TRUE when the file was written, or when the mode opens no file

    case writerMode
        MODE_W_NEW: bWritten := TRUE
        MODE_APPEND: bWritten := writeFile(@writerFile, APPEND_BASE_BYTES, WRITER_SEED)
        MODE_R_PLUS: bWritten := writeFile(@writerFile, REWRITE_BYTES, RP_OPEN_SEED)
        other: bWritten := writeFile(@writerFile, REWRITE_BYTES, WP_OLD_SEED)


PRI openNewWriter() : bReady | writeCount
' Open "w" on a new name and write two blocks: write() programs the head block, inactive, and the last block stays in the buffer.

' @returns bReady - TRUE when the writer is open and every byte was written

' Local Variables:
' @local writeCount - what write() returned

    bReady := FALSE
    oldBytes := 0                                       ' no commit of this file comes before the fault
    oldSeed := WRITER_SEED
    newBytes := FILE_BYTES
    newSeed := WRITER_SEED
    writerHndl := flash.open(@writerFile, flash.FILEMODE_WRITE)
    if isUsableHandle(writerHndl)
        fillPattern(FILE_BYTES, WRITER_SEED)
        writeCount := flash.write(writerHndl, @dataBuf, FILE_BYTES)
        bReady := writeCount == FILE_BYTES


PRI openAppendWriter() : bReady | writeCount, status, lastCount
' Open "a" on the one-block file, append and flush, then append more bytes that stay in the buffer.

' @returns bReady - TRUE when the writer is open, every byte was written and the flush succeeded

' Local Variables:
' @local writeCount - what the write() before the flush returned
' @local status - what flush() returned
' @local lastCount - what the write() after the flush returned

    bReady := FALSE
    oldBytes := APPEND_BASE_BYTES + APPEND_FIRST_BYTES  ' the flush commits the first bytes appended
    oldSeed := WRITER_SEED
    newBytes := oldBytes + APPEND_LAST_BYTES
    newSeed := WRITER_SEED
    writerHndl := flash.open(@writerFile, flash.FILEMODE_APPEND)
    if isUsableHandle(writerHndl)
        fillPattern(newBytes, WRITER_SEED)              ' the appended bytes go on with the pattern of the file
        writeCount := flash.write(writerHndl, @dataBuf + APPEND_BASE_BYTES, APPEND_FIRST_BYTES)
        status := flash.flush(writerHndl)
        lastCount := flash.write(writerHndl, @dataBuf + oldBytes, APPEND_LAST_BYTES)
        bReady := writeCount == APPEND_FIRST_BYTES and status == flash.SUCCESS and lastCount == APPEND_LAST_BYTES


PRI openRewriteWriter() : bReady | writeCount, status, position, lastCount
' Open "r+" on the one-block file, rewrite every byte and flush, then rewrite every byte again in the buffer.

' @returns bReady - TRUE when the writer is open, every byte was written, the flush succeeded and the seek reached the start

' Local Variables:
' @local writeCount - what the write() before the flush returned
' @local status - what flush() returned
' @local position - what the seek back to the start returned
' @local lastCount - what the write() after the flush returned

    bReady := FALSE
    oldBytes := REWRITE_BYTES                           ' the flush commits the first rewrite
    oldSeed := RP_FLUSH_SEED
    newBytes := REWRITE_BYTES
    newSeed := RP_FAULT_SEED
    writerHndl := flash.open(@writerFile, flash.FILEMODE_READ_EXTENDED)
    if isUsableHandle(writerHndl)
        fillPattern(REWRITE_BYTES, RP_FLUSH_SEED)
        writeCount := flash.write(writerHndl, @dataBuf, REWRITE_BYTES)
        status := flash.flush(writerHndl)
        position := flash.seek(writerHndl, 0, flash.SK_FILE_START)
        fillPattern(REWRITE_BYTES, RP_FAULT_SEED)
        lastCount := flash.write(writerHndl, @dataBuf, REWRITE_BYTES)
        bReady := writeCount == REWRITE_BYTES and status == flash.SUCCESS and position == 0 and lastCount == REWRITE_BYTES


PRI openReplaceWriter() : bReady | writeCount
' Open "w+" on the name of the one-block file and write a new file that stays in the buffer.

' @returns bReady - TRUE when the writer is open and every byte was written

' Local Variables:
' @local writeCount - what write() returned

    bReady := FALSE
    oldBytes := REWRITE_BYTES                           ' the file "w+" replaces stays as it is until the commit
    oldSeed := WP_OLD_SEED
    newBytes := WP_NEW_BYTES
    newSeed := WP_NEW_SEED
    writerHndl := flash.open(@writerFile, flash.FILEMODE_WRITE_EXTENDED)
    if isUsableHandle(writerHndl)
        fillPattern(WP_NEW_BYTES, WP_NEW_SEED)
        writeCount := flash.write(writerHndl, @dataBuf, WP_NEW_BYTES)
        bReady := writeCount == WP_NEW_BYTES


PRI injectFault(writerMode, waitNumber, allocation) | nameStatus, faultStatus, errCode, clearStatus, nextStatus
' Name the block the faulted call allocates, arm the countdown, make the call, disarm, and check the call and the next one, as one test.

' @param writerMode - the writer's mode: MODE_W_NEW, MODE_APPEND, MODE_R_PLUS or MODE_W_PLUS
' @param waitNumber - the flash wait of the faulted call that fails, counted from 1
' @param allocation - the block the faulted call's allocation takes: TAKE_FREE_BLOCK or MOVE_SECOND_HEAD

' Local Variables:
' @local nameStatus - what naming the block returned
' @local faultStatus - what the faulted call returned
' @local errCode - error() after the faulted call
' @local clearStatus - what clearing the name returned
' @local nextStatus - what the call after the fault returned

    utils.startTest(@"the faulted call returns E_FLASH_TIMEOUT, and the next call finds the filesystem not mounted")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the writer was set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        if allocation == MOVE_SECOND_HEAD
            _, _, faultBlockAddr, _ := flash.TEST_get4kBlockAndNextAtOffset(@secondFile, 0)
        else
            faultBlockAddr := findTableFreeBlock()
        nameStatus := flash.TEST_setTargetBlock(faultBlockAddr)
        ' this program runs on one cog, so no other cog calls the filesystem while the countdown is armed
        flash.TEST_armFlashFault(waitNumber)
        faultStatus := faultedCall(writerMode)          ' the armed wait reports a timeout and the call stops there, as a power loss there would stop it
        errCode := flash.error()
        flash.TEST_armFlashFault(FAULT_DISARM)          ' a countdown left armed would fire inside the recovering mount()
        clearStatus := flash.TEST_setTargetBlock(flash.NOT_ENABLED)
        nextStatus := flash.close(writerHndl)           ' the fault unmounted the filesystem and freed every handle
        debug("  block ", uhex_word_(faultBlockAddr), " named, flash wait ", udec_(waitNumber), " fails")

        utils.evaluateSubValue(faultBlockAddr <> NO_BLOCK, @"a block is named for the allocation", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(nameStatus, @"name the block", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(faultStatus, @"the faulted call", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after the faulted call", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(clearStatus, @"clear the name", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(nextStatus, @"close() after the fault", flash.E_NOT_MOUNTED)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is not mounted", FALSE)


PRI faultedCall(writerMode) : status
' Make the call of the writer's commit into which the fault is injected.

' @param writerMode - the writer's mode: MODE_W_NEW, MODE_APPEND, MODE_R_PLUS or MODE_W_PLUS
' @returns status - what the call returned

    if writerMode == MODE_R_PLUS
        status := flash.flush(writerHndl)               ' the flush rewrites the modified block
    else
        status := flash.close(writerHndl)               ' the close commits the file


PRI recoverStep(bCanMount, mountStatus, namedCheck) | bAnswer, cmErr, printBefore, printAfter
' Check can_mount() and the recovering mount() after the fault, and the block the faulted call programmed, as one test.

' @param bCanMount - the answer can_mount() must give after the fault
' @param mountStatus - the status the recovering mount() must return
' @param namedCheck - what the programmed block must hold: NAMED_NOT_CHECKED, NAMED_ERASED or NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED

' Local Variables:
' @local bAnswer - what can_mount() returned
' @local cmErr - error() after can_mount()
' @local printBefore - fingerprint of the programmed block before mount()
' @local printAfter - fingerprint of the programmed block after mount()

    utils.startTest(@"can_mount() and the recovering mount() give the pair a power loss at that step gives")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the writer was set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        ' can_mount() first reads the chip's status until its busy bit clears, so the erase or program the
        ' fault left running is finished before any block is read
        bAnswer := flash.can_mount()
        cmErr := flash.error()
        utils.evaluateSubValue(bAnswer, @"can_mount()", bCanMount)
        ' can_mount() leaves E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED when mount() would remove a block, and SUCCESS otherwise
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(cmErr, @"error() after can_mount()", mountStatus)
        printBefore := 0
        if namedCheck <> NAMED_NOT_CHECKED
            printBefore := checkNamedBlock(namedCheck)
        mountAndCheck(mountStatus)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is mounted", TRUE)
        if namedCheck <> NAMED_NOT_CHECKED
            printAfter := blockPrint(faultBlockAddr)
            utils.evaluateSubValue(printAfter, @"mount() leaves the half-written block as it was", printBefore)


PRI checkNamedBlock(namedCheck) : blockCrc | pBlock
' Check that the block the faulted call programmed holds what the fault left, and fingerprint it.

' @param namedCheck - what the programmed block must hold: NAMED_ERASED or NAMED_PART_PROGRAMMED
' @returns blockCrc - CRC of the 4KB block, or the error code when it could not be read

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - the hook's buffer address, or the error code when the block could not be read

    pBlock := readInto(faultBlockAddr, @copyBuf)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(pBlock > 0, @"the half-written block was read back", TRUE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(cycleOf(@copyBuf), @"the half-written block is inactive", CYCLE_INACTIVE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(LONG[@copyBuf + flash.BLOCK_SIZE - CRC_BYTES], @"the last page of the half-written block is still erased", ERASED_LONG)
    if namedCheck == NAMED_ERASED
        utils.evaluateSubValue(LONG[@copyBuf], @"the first page of the half-written block is still erased", ERASED_LONG)
    else
        utils.evaluateSubValue(LONG[@copyBuf] <> ERASED_LONG, @"the first page of the half-written block was programmed", TRUE)
    blockCrc := blockPrint(faultBlockAddr)


PRI faultedMountStep(recoveryWait, retryStatus) | bAnswer, cmErr, status, errCode
' Check that a mount() whose own flash wait fails returns E_FLASH_TIMEOUT and stays unmounted, and that the next mount() recovers, as one test.

' @param recoveryWait - the flash wait of mount() that fails, counted from 1
' @param retryStatus - the status the next mount() must return

' Local Variables:
' @local bAnswer - what can_mount() returned
' @local cmErr - error() after can_mount()
' @local status - what the faulted mount() returned
' @local errCode - error() after the faulted mount()

    utils.startTest(@"a mount() whose own flash wait fails returns E_FLASH_TIMEOUT and stays unmounted, and the next mount() recovers")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the writer was set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        bAnswer := flash.can_mount()                    ' its status read lets the activate the fault left running finish
        cmErr := flash.error()
        utils.evaluateSubValue(bAnswer, @"can_mount() with an orphan body block", FALSE)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(cmErr, @"error() after can_mount()", flash.E_BAD_BLOCKS_REMOVED)

        flash.TEST_armFlashFault(recoveryWait)
        status := flash.mount()                         ' the armed wait reports a timeout inside mount()
        errCode := flash.error()
        flash.TEST_armFlashFault(FAULT_DISARM)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"the faulted mount()", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after the faulted mount()", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is not mounted", FALSE)

        mountAndCheck(retryStatus)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is mounted", TRUE)


PRI unmountedFaultStep() | bAnswer, errCode, freeAddr, printBefore, status, printNow
' Check that can_mount() whose flash wait fails returns FALSE with E_FLASH_TIMEOUT, that block programs stay refused, and that mount() recovers, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local bAnswer - what can_mount() returned
' @local errCode - error() after can_mount()
' @local freeAddr - a block no file uses
' @local printBefore - fingerprint of that block before the program
' @local status - what the program returned
' @local printNow - fingerprint of that block after it

    utils.startTest(@"can_mount() whose flash wait fails returns FALSE with E_FLASH_TIMEOUT; block programs stay refused until mount()")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        flash.TEST_armFlashFault(WAIT_READY_CHECK)
        bAnswer := flash.can_mount()                    ' the armed wait reports a timeout, so no block is checked
        errCode := flash.error()
        flash.TEST_armFlashFault(FAULT_DISARM)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(bAnswer, @"can_mount()", FALSE)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after can_mount()", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is not mounted", FALSE)

        freeAddr := findFreeBlock(NO_BLOCK)
        printBefore := blockPrint(freeAddr)
        bytefill(@copyBuf, 0, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
        ' the device fault stays raised until mount() or format(), so this program sends nothing to the flash
        status := flash.TEST_changeBlock(flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM, freeAddr, @copyBuf, CYCLE_FIRST)
        printNow := blockPrint(freeAddr)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"program a free block while the fault is raised", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(printNow, @"the free block is unchanged", printBefore)

        mountAndCheck(flash.SUCCESS)                    ' mount() waits for the chip, clears the fault and mounts
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is mounted", TRUE)


PRI guardRefusalStep(blockOp, pTitle) | crcStatus, crcBefore, refusedBefore, status, errCode, nextStatus, crcNow
' Refuse a program below the filesystem while it is mounted, check the fault exit and the sector below, and recover, as one test.

' @param blockOp - the primitive TEST_programBelowFs() calls: flash.TEST_BLK_PROGRAM or flash.TEST_BLK_CORRUPT
' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test

' Local Variables:
' @local crcStatus - what the read of the sector below the filesystem returned
' @local crcBefore - CRC of that sector before the refused call
' @local refusedBefore - refused block programs before the call
' @local status - what the refused call returned
' @local errCode - error() after the refused call
' @local nextStatus - what the call after the refusal returned
' @local crcNow - CRC of that sector after the refused call

    utils.startTest(pTitle)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        crcStatus, crcBefore := flash.TEST_readBelowFsCrc()
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(crcStatus, @"read the sector below the filesystem before the call", flash.SUCCESS)
        refusedBefore := flash.TEST_refusedBlockCount()
        ' the primitive refuses the block below the filesystem before any command, and the refusal raises the device fault
        status := flash.TEST_programBelowFs(blockOp)
        errCode := flash.error()
        nextStatus := flash.close(secondHndl)           ' the fault unmounted the filesystem and freed every handle
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"the refused call", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after the refused call", flash.E_FLASH_TIMEOUT)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.TEST_refusedBlockCount(), @"refused block programs", refusedBefore + ONE_REFUSAL)
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(nextStatus, @"close() after the refusal", flash.E_NOT_MOUNTED)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is not mounted", FALSE)

        crcStatus, crcNow := flash.TEST_readBelowFsCrc()
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(crcStatus, @"read the sector below the filesystem after the call", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(crcNow, @"the sector below the filesystem is unchanged", crcBefore)

        mountAndCheck(flash.SUCCESS)                    ' mount() clears the fault and mounts
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is mounted", TRUE)


PRI setUpSecond(bKeepMounted) | status, bSecond, bReady
' Format and write the second file, then open it for reading or unmount, as one test; bSetUp tells the later steps whether it worked.

' @param bKeepMounted - TRUE to keep the filesystem mounted with the second file open for reading, FALSE to unmount

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what format() and unmount() returned
' @local bSecond - TRUE when the second file was written
' @local bReady - TRUE when the second file is open for reading, or the filesystem was unmounted

    utils.startTest(@"set up: write the second file, then open it for reading or unmount")
    bSetUp := FALSE
    status := flash.format()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"format()", flash.SUCCESS)
    bSetUp := status == flash.SUCCESS
    bSecond := writeFile(@secondFile, FILE_BYTES, SECOND_SEED)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSecond, @"the second file is written", TRUE)
    if bKeepMounted
        secondHndl := flash.open(@secondFile, flash.FILEMODE_READ)
        bReady := isUsableHandle(secondHndl)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(bReady, @"the second file is open for reading", TRUE)
    else
        status := flash.unmount()
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"unmount()", flash.SUCCESS)
        bReady := status == flash.SUCCESS
    bSetUp := bSetUp and bSecond and bReady


PRI committedStep(writerOutcome) | fileCount
' Check that the committed data verifies: the second file whole, and the writer's file as its last commit or the faulted commit left it, as one test.

' @param writerOutcome - what the writer's file must hold: WRITER_GONE, WRITER_OLD or WRITER_NEW

' Local Variables:
' @local fileCount - files the filesystem reports

    utils.startTest(@"the committed data verifies")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        utils.evaluateSubValue(fileMatches(@secondFile, FILE_BYTES, SECOND_SEED), @"the second file reads back whole", TRUE)
        _, _, fileCount := flash.stats()
        case writerOutcome
            WRITER_GONE:
                utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.exists(@writerFile), @"the writer's file does not exist", FALSE)
                utils.evaluateSubValue(fileCount, @"files in the filesystem", FILES_WITHOUT_WRITER)
            WRITER_OLD:
                ' one seed and one size: every byte is the last commit's, none is the faulted commit's
                utils.evaluateSubValue(fileMatches(@writerFile, oldBytes, oldSeed), @"the writer's file reads back exactly as its last commit left it", TRUE)
                utils.evaluateSubValue(fileCount, @"files in the filesystem", FILES_WITH_WRITER)
            other:
                ' one seed and one size: every byte is the faulted commit's, none is the last commit's
                utils.evaluateSubValue(fileMatches(@writerFile, newBytes, newSeed), @"the writer's file reads back exactly with the faulted commit", TRUE)
                utils.evaluateSubValue(fileCount, @"files in the filesystem", FILES_WITH_WRITER)


PRI setUpFiles() | status, pBuff, pBuff2, bKeep, bTarget, bCrc, bIdsFree
' Format, write the three files, find their blocks and unmount, as one test; bSetUp tells the later steps whether it worked.

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what format() and unmount() returned
' @local pBuff - head block buffer the block hook returned
' @local pBuff2 - body block buffer the block hook returned
' @local bKeep - TRUE when the untouched file was written and has a head and a body block
' @local bTarget - TRUE when the target file was written and has a head and a body block
' @local bCrc - TRUE when the one-block file was written and has one block
' @local bIdsFree - TRUE when no block of the three files uses the orphan ID

    utils.startTest(@"set up: format, write three files, find their blocks and unmount")
    bSetUp := FALSE
    bIdsFree := TRUE
    status := flash.format()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"format()", flash.SUCCESS)
    bSetUp := status == flash.SUCCESS

    bKeep := writeFile(@keepFile, FILE_BYTES, KEEP_SEED)
    pBuff, pBuff2, _, _ := flash.TEST_get4kBlockAndNextAtOffset(@keepFile, 0)
    bKeep := bKeep and pBuff <> 0 and pBuff2 <> 0
    if bKeep
        bIdsFree := bIdsFree and idOf(pBuff) <> ORPHAN_ID and idOf(pBuff2) <> ORPHAN_ID
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bKeep, @"the untouched file is written with a head and a body block", TRUE)

    bTarget := writeFile(@targetFile, FILE_BYTES, TARGET_SEED)
    pBuff, pBuff2, targetHeadAddr, targetBodyAddr := flash.TEST_get4kBlockAndNextAtOffset(@targetFile, 0)
    bTarget := bTarget and pBuff <> 0 and pBuff2 <> 0
    if bTarget
        targetHeadId := idOf(pBuff)
        targetBodyId := idOf(pBuff2)
        bIdsFree := bIdsFree and targetHeadId <> ORPHAN_ID and targetBodyId <> ORPHAN_ID
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bTarget, @"the target file is written with a head and a body block", TRUE)

    bCrc := writeFile(@crcFile, ONE_BLOCK_BYTES, CRC_SEED)
    pBuff, pBuff2, crcHeadAddr, _ := flash.TEST_get4kBlockAndNextAtOffset(@crcFile, 0)
    bCrc := bCrc and pBuff <> 0 and pBuff2 == 0
    if bCrc
        bIdsFree := bIdsFree and idOf(pBuff) <> ORPHAN_ID
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bCrc, @"the one-block file is written with one block", TRUE)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bIdsFree, @"no block of the three files uses the orphan ID", TRUE)

    status := flash.unmount()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"unmount()", flash.SUCCESS)
    bSetUp := bSetUp and bKeep and bTarget and bCrc and bIdsFree and status == flash.SUCCESS
    touchedCount := 0


PRI canMountStep(pTitle, bExpected) | bCanMount, touchIdx
' Call can_mount() with the filesystem unmounted, and check its answer and that every touched block is unchanged, as one test.

' @param pTitle - address of zstring naming the test
' @param bExpected - the answer can_mount() must give

' Local Variables:
' @local bCanMount - what can_mount() returned
' @local touchIdx - number of the touched block being compared

    utils.startTest(pTitle)
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        recordPrints(@printsBefore)
        bCanMount := flash.can_mount()
        recordPrints(@printsAfter)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(bCanMount, @"can_mount()", bExpected)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.mounted(), @"the filesystem is still not mounted", FALSE)
        repeat touchedCount with touchIdx
            utils.evaluateSubValue(printsAfter[touchIdx], @"a touched block is unchanged by can_mount()", printsBefore[touchIdx])


PRI survivorsStep(bOneBlockFileKept) | fileCount
' Check the files that must survive read back whole, and the file count, as one test.

' @param bOneBlockFileKept - TRUE when the one-block file must survive, FALSE when mount() removed it

' Local Variables:
' @local fileCount - files the filesystem reports

    utils.startTest(@"the files that must survive read back whole")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        utils.evaluateSubValue(fileMatches(@keepFile, FILE_BYTES, KEEP_SEED), @"the untouched file reads back whole", TRUE)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(fileMatches(@targetFile, FILE_BYTES, TARGET_SEED), @"the target file reads back whole", TRUE)
        _, _, fileCount := flash.stats()
        if bOneBlockFileKept
            utils.evaluateSubValue(fileMatches(@crcFile, ONE_BLOCK_BYTES, CRC_SEED), @"the one-block file reads back whole", TRUE)
            utils.evaluateSubValue(fileCount, @"files in the filesystem", FILE_COUNT)
        else
            utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.exists(@crcFile), @"the one-block file is gone", FALSE)
            utils.evaluateSubValue(fileCount, @"files in the filesystem", FILE_COUNT - 1)


PRI secondMountStep() | status
' Unmount, then check can_mount() is TRUE and mount() returns SUCCESS, as one test.

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what unmount() returned

    utils.startTest(@"a second mount is clean: can_mount() TRUE, then mount() SUCCESS")
    utils.evaluateSubValue(bSetUp, @"the files were set up", TRUE)
    if bSetUp
        status := flash.unmount()
        utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"unmount()", flash.SUCCESS)
        utils.evaluateSubValue(flash.can_mount(), @"can_mount()", TRUE)
        mountAndCheck(flash.SUCCESS)


PRI mountAndCheck(expectedStatus) | status, errCode
' Call mount() and check its status and the error() it leaves.

' @param expectedStatus - the status mount() must return and leave in error()

' Local Variables:
' @local status - what mount() returned
' @local errCode - error() after mount()

    status := flash.mount()
    errCode := flash.error()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(status, @"mount()", expectedStatus)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after mount()", expectedStatus)


PRI checkStatus(returned, expectedCode) | errCode
' Check the status a call returned and the error() it left are both a code.

' @param returned - the status the call returned
' @param expectedCode - the code the call must return and leave in error()

' Local Variables:
' @local errCode - error() as the call left it

    errCode := flash.error()
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(returned, @"status returned", expectedCode)
    utils.evaluateSubStatus(errCode, @"error() after the call", expectedCode)


PRI setTouched(firstAddr, secondAddr, thirdAddr, fourthAddr)
' Record the blocks a group touched, skipping NO_BLOCK.

' @param firstAddr - a touched block, or NO_BLOCK
' @param secondAddr - a touched block, or NO_BLOCK
' @param thirdAddr - a touched block, or NO_BLOCK
' @param fourthAddr - a touched block, or NO_BLOCK

    touchedCount := 0
    addTouched(firstAddr)
    addTouched(secondAddr)
    addTouched(thirdAddr)
    addTouched(fourthAddr)


PRI addTouched(blockAddr)
' Add a block to the touched list, unless it is NO_BLOCK or the list is full.

' @param blockAddr - a touched block, or NO_BLOCK

    if blockAddr <> NO_BLOCK and touchedCount < MAX_TOUCHED
        touchedAddr[touchedCount++] := blockAddr


PRI recordPrints(pPrints) | touchIdx
' Fingerprint every touched block into a list.

' @param pPrints - address of a list of MAX_TOUCHED longs

' Local Variables:
' @local touchIdx - number of the touched block being fingerprinted

    repeat touchedCount with touchIdx
        LONG[pPrints][touchIdx] := blockPrint(touchedAddr[touchIdx])


PRI blockPrint(blockAddr) : blockCrc | pBlock
' Fingerprint a whole block: a CRC over all its bytes.

' @param blockAddr - the block to fingerprint
' @returns blockCrc - CRC of the 4KB block, or the error code when it could not be read

' Local Variables:
' @local pBlock - address of the block read

    pBlock := flash.TEST_readBlock(blockAddr)
    if pBlock > 0
        blockCrc := getcrc(pBlock, BLOCK_CRC_POLY, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
    else
        blockCrc := pBlock


PRI readInto(blockAddr, pDest) : pBlock
' Read a block into a buffer of the test.

' @param blockAddr - the block to read
' @param pDest - address of a buffer of flash.BLOCK_SIZE bytes, cleared when the block could not be read
' @returns pBlock - the hook's buffer address, or the error code when the block could not be read

    pBlock := flash.TEST_readBlock(blockAddr)
    if pBlock > 0
        bytemove(pDest, pBlock, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)
    else
        bytefill(pDest, 0, flash.BLOCK_SIZE)


PRI findFreeBlock(excludeAddr) : blockAddr | scanAddr, pBlock
' Find the lowest block whose lifecycle is not active, other than one block to leave out.

' @param excludeAddr - a block not to return, or NO_BLOCK
' @returns blockAddr - the block found, or NO_BLOCK when every block is active

' Local Variables:
' @local scanAddr - the block being looked at
' @local pBlock - address of that block as read

    blockAddr := NO_BLOCK
    repeat flash.BLOCKS with scanAddr
        if scanAddr <> excludeAddr
            pBlock := flash.TEST_readBlock(scanAddr)
            if pBlock > 0
                ifnot isActive(BYTE[pBlock].[CYCLE_TOP..CYCLE_BOTTOM])
                    blockAddr := scanAddr
                    quit


PRI findTableFreeBlock() : blockAddr | scanAddr
' Find the lowest block the mounted filesystem's table holds as B_FREE: a block no file and no open writer uses.

' @returns blockAddr - the block found, or NO_BLOCK when no block is free

' Local Variables:
' @local scanAddr - the block being looked at

    blockAddr := NO_BLOCK
    repeat flash.BLOCKS with scanAddr
        if flash.TEST_blockStateOf(scanAddr) == flash.B_FREE
            blockAddr := scanAddr
            quit


PRI cycleOf(pBlock) : cycle
' Give the lifecycle of a block image.

' @param pBlock - address of a block image
' @returns cycle - the lifecycle bits

    cycle := BYTE[pBlock].[CYCLE_TOP..CYCLE_BOTTOM]


PRI idOf(pBlock) : blockId
' Give the block ID of a block image.

' @param pBlock - address of a block image
' @returns blockId - the ID the block carries

    blockId := LONG[pBlock].[ID_TOP..ID_BOTTOM]


PRI fieldOf(pBlock, fieldSel) : fieldValue
' Give one control field of a block image.

' @param pBlock - address of a block image
' @param fieldSel - the field: FIELD_THIS_ID, FIELD_NEXT or FIELD_DATA_OFFSET
' @returns fieldValue - the value the block holds in that field

    case fieldSel
        FIELD_THIS_ID: fieldValue := LONG[pBlock].[ID_TOP..ID_BOTTOM]
        FIELD_NEXT: fieldValue := LONG[pBlock].[NEXT_TOP..NEXT_BOTTOM]
        other: fieldValue := LONG[pBlock][DATA_OFFSET_LONG].[DATA_OFFSET_TOP..DATA_OFFSET_BOTTOM]


PRI setField(pBlock, fieldSel, fieldValue)
' Change one control field of a block image.

' @param pBlock - address of a block image
' @param fieldSel - the field: FIELD_THIS_ID, FIELD_NEXT or FIELD_DATA_OFFSET
' @param fieldValue - the value to place in that field

    case fieldSel
        FIELD_THIS_ID: LONG[pBlock].[ID_TOP..ID_BOTTOM] := fieldValue
        FIELD_NEXT: LONG[pBlock].[NEXT_TOP..NEXT_BOTTOM] := fieldValue
        other: LONG[pBlock][DATA_OFFSET_LONG].[DATA_OFFSET_TOP..DATA_OFFSET_BOTTOM] := fieldValue


PRI nextCycle(cycle) : newerCycle
' Give the active lifecycle that is newer than an active lifecycle.

' @param cycle - an active lifecycle
' @returns newerCycle - the lifecycle a replacement of that block is given

    case cycle
        CYCLE_FIRST: newerCycle := CYCLE_SECOND
        CYCLE_SECOND: newerCycle := CYCLE_THIRD
        other: newerCycle := CYCLE_FIRST


PRI isActive(cycle) : bActive
' Tell whether a lifecycle is active: exactly one zero bit.

' @param cycle - lifecycle bits
' @returns bActive - TRUE when the lifecycle is active

    bActive := (ones cycle) == ACTIVE_ONES


PRI isCancelled(cycle) : bCancelled
' Tell whether a lifecycle is cancelled: two or three zero bits.

' @param cycle - lifecycle bits
' @returns bCancelled - TRUE when the lifecycle is cancelled

    bCancelled := (ones cycle) <= CANCELLED_MAX_ONES


PRI isCrcGood(pBlock) : bGood
' Tell whether the CRC stored at the end of a block image matches the bytes before it.

' @param pBlock - address of a block image
' @returns bGood - TRUE when the stored CRC matches

    bGood := LONG[pBlock + flash.BLOCK_SIZE - CRC_BYTES] == getcrc(pBlock, BLOCK_CRC_POLY, flash.BLOCK_SIZE - CRC_BYTES)


PRI dataMatches(pFirst, pSecond) : bSame | byteIdx
' Tell whether two block images hold the same bytes from the second byte to the CRC: everything but the lifecycle byte and the CRC.

' @param pFirst - address of a block image
' @param pSecond - address of a block image
' @returns bSame - TRUE when the bytes agree

' Local Variables:
' @local byteIdx - offset of the byte being compared

    bSame := TRUE
    repeat byteIdx from FIRST_DATA_BYTE to flash.BLOCK_SIZE - CRC_BYTES - 1
        if BYTE[pFirst][byteIdx] <> BYTE[pSecond][byteIdx]
            bSame := FALSE
            quit


PRI writeFile(pName, byteCount, seed) : bWritten | handle, writeCount, status
' Write a file of a pattern: each byte is the seed plus its offset, kept to one byte.

' @param pName - address of zstring with the file name
' @param byteCount - bytes to write
' @param seed - value of the first byte
' @returns bWritten - TRUE when every byte was written and the close succeeded

' Local Variables:
' @local handle - handle of the file
' @local writeCount - what write() returned
' @local status - what close() returned

    bWritten := FALSE
    fillPattern(byteCount, seed)
    handle := flash.open(pName, flash.FILEMODE_WRITE)
    if isUsableHandle(handle)
        writeCount := flash.write(handle, @dataBuf, byteCount)
        status := utils.closeHandle(handle)
        bWritten := writeCount == byteCount and status == flash.SUCCESS


PRI fileMatches(pName, byteCount, seed) : bMatches | handle, readCount, byteIdx
' Read a whole file and tell whether it holds the pattern writeFile() writes, and nothing more.

' @param pName - address of zstring with the file name
' @param byteCount - bytes the file must hold
' @param seed - value of its first byte
' @returns bMatches - TRUE when the size and every byte match

' Local Variables:
' @local handle - handle of the file
' @local readCount - what read() returned
' @local byteIdx - offset of the byte being compared

    bMatches := FALSE
    if flash.file_size(pName) == byteCount
        bytefill(@dataBuf, 0, byteCount)
        handle := flash.open(pName, flash.FILEMODE_READ)
        if isUsableHandle(handle)
            readCount := flash.read(handle, @dataBuf, byteCount)
            utils.closeHandle(handle)
            bMatches := readCount == byteCount
            repeat byteCount with byteIdx
                if dataBuf[byteIdx] <> (seed + byteIdx) & BYTE_MASK
                    bMatches := FALSE
                    quit


PRI fillMatches(pName, byteCount, fillValue) : bMatches | handle, readCount, chunkBytes, doneBytes, byteIdx
' Read a whole file through the data buffer, one buffer at a time, and tell whether every byte is the fill value and the size matches.

' @param pName - address of zstring with the file name
' @param byteCount - bytes the file must hold
' @param fillValue - the value every byte must hold
' @returns bMatches - TRUE when the size and every byte match

' Local Variables:
' @local handle - handle of the file
' @local readCount - what read() returned for one buffer
' @local chunkBytes - bytes asked for in one read()
' @local doneBytes - bytes read and compared so far
' @local byteIdx - offset of the byte being compared

    bMatches := FALSE
    if flash.file_size(pName) == byteCount
        handle := flash.open(pName, flash.FILEMODE_READ)
        if isUsableHandle(handle)
            bMatches := TRUE
            doneBytes := 0
            repeat while bMatches and doneBytes < byteCount
                chunkBytes := (byteCount - doneBytes) <# FILE_BYTES
                bytefill(@dataBuf, !fillValue, chunkBytes)      ' a byte read() did not write cannot match
                readCount := flash.read(handle, @dataBuf, chunkBytes)
                bMatches := readCount == chunkBytes
                repeat chunkBytes with byteIdx
                    if dataBuf[byteIdx] <> fillValue
                        bMatches := FALSE
                        quit
                doneBytes += chunkBytes
            utils.closeHandle(handle)


PRI fillPattern(byteCount, seed) | byteIdx
' Fill the data buffer with the pattern: each byte is the seed plus its offset, kept to one byte.

' @param byteCount - bytes to fill
' @param seed - value of the first byte

' Local Variables:
' @local byteIdx - offset of the byte being filled

    repeat byteCount with byteIdx
        dataBuf[byteIdx] := (seed + byteIdx) & BYTE_MASK


PRI isUsableHandle(handle) : bUsable
' Tell whether a value is one of the handles open() hands out.

' @param handle - value open() returned
' @returns bUsable - TRUE when the value is inside the range of handles

    bUsable := handle >= utils.FIRST_HANDLE and handle < utils.FIRST_HANDLE + flash.MAX_FILES_OPEN


